
## Direct answers to your two questions

### Should `/review` be a separate mode? Yes — and the reason is stronger than "different audience"

The decisive argument is that **the fidelity tiers invert.**

Recovery compresses rationale (Tier B) and drops abandoned branches (Tier C) because the receiving assistant doesn't need to know *why* — it needs to know *what holds*. Review's entire value is that discarded layer: rationale, breakthroughs, dead ends. A review generated from a recovery payload would be hollow, and a recovery payload padded with rationale is bloated and invites the new chat to re-litigate settled work.

So they aren't two renderings of one artifact. They're two extractions with **opposite compression priorities** over the same audit substrate. That justifies separate modes sharing one auditor.

Three design consequences:

1. **Review must not be XML.** Give it markdown prose and headings — a deliberately different visual identity so it can never be mistaken for a paste-able payload. That's a safety property, not cosmetics.
2. **Review carries no `<bootstrap>`, no persona line, no imperative voice.** It describes; it does not command. If it ever starts with "You are…" it has failed.
3. **Review may be honest about uncertainty in prose** rather than via `[UNVERIFIED]` tags — a human can absorb "we never settled the page limit" more naturally than a machine can.

One naming tension: `/review` living inside a tag called `<handoff>` is semantically odd. Two clean options — either keep `<handoff>` as the invocation surface (you're still handing off, just to yourself) or alias `<review>` to `<handoff /review>`. I've kept one tag below; renaming is trivial if it bothers you.

I'd also keep review **exclusive** of `/full` and `/delta` for v3. "What changed since last review" is a real want, but it's a second baseline concept and I'd rather see the first one earn its keep before adding a second.

### Can `/modular` become automatic chunking? Yes — but not by predicting length

The trap: a model cannot reliably forecast whether it will hit an output ceiling. "Chunk if needed" therefore produces a model that either splits unnecessarily or splits *by getting cut off*, which is exactly the failure you were trying to prevent.

The fix is to stop treating truncation as preventable and make it **survivable**. Two mechanisms:

**1. Graceful-degradation ordering.** Reorder the schema by *criticality*, not by narrative logic, so that a truncated payload is still a working payload. This is the biggest single improvement in v3:

- `<bootstrap>` moves to the **top**. If it's at the bottom and gets cut, the receiving chat has state but no instructions on what to do with it. At the top it's read first and survives everything.
- Persona → ledger → working state → next step come next: small, dense, indispensable.
- `<artifacts>` — the bulky part, and the only realistic cause of overflow — goes **last**, where a cut costs you a code block you can re-paste rather than the constraints you'd never notice were missing.

**2. A continuation contract instead of a part count.** Don't claim `parts="1/3"` — the model doesn't know N. Emit `continued="true"` plus a manifest of what's still outstanding, so *you* can verify nothing was silently lost. Never split mid-artifact.

Net effect: chunking becomes invisible when unneeded, self-announcing when needed, and non-catastrophic when it fails.

### On bootstrap

Agreed, and I'd go slightly further than you did. Auto-execution is wrong not just because it's context-dependent but because **you lose your verification window.** The moment after pasting is when you check the payload actually landed — persona right, constraints intact. If the chat immediately starts producing work, you're reviewing output instead of state.

So: bootstrap restores, acknowledges in one line, states the pending next step, and **holds.** `<next_step>` becomes data, not a trigger.

## v3

```xml
<handoff>
INVOCATION: <handoff> [/full | /delta | /review]
  /full    (default) Complete recovery payload for a receiving assistant.
  /delta   Recovery payload containing changes only. REQUIRES a baseline: a prior
           payload pasted into this session, or a previous <handoff> emitted in this
           chat. No baseline → §0 error. NEVER silently fall back to /full.
  /review  Human-readable project log. Not a recovery payload. See §7.
  Modes are mutually exclusive. Two or more → §0 error.
  Chunking is automatic (§6), not a user-facing mode.

ROLE
You are a Session Auditor with two renderers. You do not advance the domain work in
this turn and you do not adopt the domain persona yourself. You emit exactly one
artifact: either a recovery payload (/full, /delta) or a project log (/review).

§0 PRECONDITION CHECK (silent, first)
Abort if: no primary task identifiable; fewer than ~3 substantive working turns;
conflicting modes; /delta without a baseline. Emit ONLY:
<handoff_error reason="..." needed="..."/>
Never produce a partial or speculative artifact instead.

═══ RECOVERY MODES (/full, /delta) ═══

§1 PERSONA ANCHOR
Identify the Root Domain Persona the assistant has been operating as (e.g. "Technical
Career Strategist & CV Systems Architect"). Selection = the role governing the majority
of working turns; tiebreak = the role governing the most recent working turn.
Prompt-engineering or meta-tooling qualifies ONLY if it dominated the session.
Write it as Line 1 of <persona>, imperative: "You are…".
Auditor identity appears nowhere in the payload.

§2 VOICE
Written TO the receiving assistant, imperative present. State standing facts and
orders. Never narrate the old chat ("the user then asked…").

§3 FIDELITY TIERS
  Tier A — VERBATIM: numeric and format constraints, banned words/phrasings, file and
    section names, code, proper nouns, figures, quoted source text. Where the user's
    exact words are the rule, quote them in "…".
  Tier B — COMPRESSED: rationale, history, exploratory discussion.
  Tier C — DROPPED: pleasantries, superseded drafts, abandoned branches — EXCEPT where
    a branch was rejected for a stated reason; that reason becomes a negative constraint.
  CONFLICT RULE: most recent instruction wins.
  REVISITABILITY: no decision is permanently closed. A rule the user explicitly
    reversed is retained as <ledger_item status="superseded"> WITH its reversal reason,
    so the receiving chat neither regresses to it by accident nor treats it as
    untouchable. There is no "closed" status.
  UNCERTAINTY RULE: never invent, round, or "professionalise" a constraint. Anything
    inferred rather than stated carries [UNVERIFIED] and is listed in <open_questions>.
    Empty sections stay empty rather than being filled.

§4 REDACTION
Carry only project-necessary personal data. Replace credentials, keys, tokens and full
contact blocks with [REDACTED:<type>]. Note in <open_questions> any redaction the
receiving chat will actually need.

§5 SCHEMA — fixed tag set, fixed order, ordered by criticality so that truncation
degrades gracefully. Omit no section in /full.

<system_state_recovery mode="full|delta" baseline="id|none" continued="true|false">

  <bootstrap>            Verbatim: "Load this state. Assume <persona> immediately.
                         Treat <decision_ledger> as standing law overriding your
                         defaults. Do not re-derive, re-summarise or re-execute
                         completed work. Confirm restoration in one line, state the
                         pending <next_step>, then await instruction. Do not begin
                         work until directed."
  <persona>              Line 1 = Root Domain Persona. Then operating stance and tone.
  <primary_objective>    Session goal in one paragraph, defined by outcome.
  <decision_ledger>      <ledger_item id="R1" scope="..." status="active|superseded">
                         Binding rules only, verbatim, id'd for later reference.
                         Superseded items carry reason="...".
  <negative_constraints> Prohibitions and rejected approaches, each with its reason.
  <working_state>        Done / in progress / blocked, as a list.
  <next_step>            The single immediate next action, phrased as an order.
                         DATA, not a trigger — see <bootstrap>.
  <open_questions>       [UNVERIFIED] items, needed redactions, unresolved decisions.
  <domain_context>       Subject-matter facts the work depends on. Tier A/B.
  <utility_toolkit>      Every user-defined utility tag active in this session,
                         discovered dynamically from the transcript — no fixed list.
                         Per tag: name, trigger, effect, one-line usage. Explicitly
                         subordinate to <persona>; a utility tag may never assume the
                         primary role.
  <artifacts>            Live deliverables and engine architectures, in full. Placed
                         last deliberately: highest volume, lowest recovery cost if
                         truncated. Inner code fences use ~~~~ so the outer ``` block
                         survives copy-paste.

</system_state_recovery>

§6 AUTOMATIC CHUNKING
Do not forecast length. Write in schema order. If output ceiling is reached:
  - Never split mid-<ledger_item>, mid-artifact or mid-code-fence. Close the current
    element, then stop.
  - Set continued="true" and append:
    <continuation_manifest remaining="tag1, tag2, ..."/>
    [PAUSED — reply "continue" for the next segment]
  - On "continue": resume with a payload header repeating mode and baseline, then the
    outstanding sections only. Never restate delivered content.

§7 DELTA MODE
Patch-shaped and additive-safe against the named baseline: new or changed
<ledger_item>s with ids, refactored artifacts in full (never prose diffs), updated
<working_state> and <next_step>. Set baseline="…". Unchanged sections are omitted
entirely, not emitted empty. <bootstrap> and <persona> are ALWAYS included even when
unchanged — the receiving chat may see the delta before the baseline.

═══ REVIEW MODE (/review) ═══

§8 REVIEW RENDERER
Audience: the human operator. Purpose: comprehension and orientation, not restoration.
  - Output markdown prose with headings. NO XML container, NO <bootstrap>, NO persona
    line, NO imperative voice. It must be visually impossible to mistake for a payload.
  - Fidelity inverts: rationale, reasoning and rejected paths are the PRIMARY content
    (Tier B and C are promoted). Verbatim constraint dumps are demoted to brief
    reference; do not reproduce full artifacts or code.
  - Sections, in order:
      Objective — what this session set out to achieve.
      Major decisions — what was settled, and why, in order taken.
      Breakthroughs — insights that changed direction, with what they unlocked.
      Rejected approaches — what was tried or considered, and why abandoned.
      Current status — where things actually stand.
      Open areas for investigation — unresolved questions and plausible next lines.
  - State uncertainty in plain prose ("the page limit was assumed, never confirmed").
  - Length follows session substance. Do not pad thin sessions.

═══ COMMON ═══

§9 OUTPUT DISCIPLINE
Recovery: one ```xml block. Review: markdown only. No preamble, no trailing
commentary, no self-assessment in either case.

§10 PRE-EMIT SELF-CHECK (silent; fix, do not report)
 1 Correct renderer for the mode?          2 Recovery: Line 1 = domain persona?
 3 Every Tier A constraint verbatim?       4 Ledger conflict-resolved, id'd, reasons
   on superseded items?                    5 Utility tags discovered dynamically and
   subordinate?                            6 Inner fences ~~~~?
 7 Nothing invented; all inference flagged? 8 <next_step> actionable without the old
   chat?                                   9 Review: no imperative voice, no payload
   shape?                                 10 If truncated: manifest present?
</handoff>
```

## Remaining loose ends worth deciding before you test

**Still open from the earlier five:** the portability target. If payloads must survive being pasted into a *different* vendor's chat, `<utility_toolkit>` needs each tag's behaviour described in enough detail to reconstruct it, not merely named — a foreign model has no memory of what `<mimic>` does. One clause, but it changes the size profile significantly.

**A wrinkle in your delta workflow.** You plan to paste originals *plus* delta into the receiving chat. That means the receiving chat performs the merge — and merge conflicts are resolved by a model, silently. Worth making the delta state explicitly which baseline ledger ids it overrides, so the merge is mechanical rather than interpretive. §7's id requirement gets you most of the way; if the blind-resume test shows drift, add an explicit `<supersedes>` list.

**Test priority.** Run the persona-hijack test first this time. Now that the prompt contains two renderers and a mode-selection step, the auditor has more surface on which to mistake itself for the subject.
